From b9a4e2b8874ba614b13277ddf6e472e1d57b76ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 11:08:45 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] MSL: Fix robustBufferAccess2 loads, bounds and image atomic
 stores

Compile errors in the MSL of DXVK, gamescope and Slang shaders with robustBufferAccess2:
- The out-of-bounds select of a load used the loaded value in the physical layout of the buffer:
  'packed_float3x4' selected against 'float3x4(0)' ("incompatible operand types", gamescope's
  'vec4(color, 1) * u_ctm[colorspaceLayerIdx]'), row-major matrices untransposed. Convert the value to
  the logical type first (unpack, transpose) and drop the physical layout from the expression.
- Struct values were zeroed with 'S(0)' ("no matching conversion for functional-style cast from
  'int'", Slang's _MatrixStorage_float4x4_ColMajor* wrappers): value-initialize them ('S{}'); pointers
  are zeroed with nullptr. Arrays cannot be selected (arrays in buffers are C arrays): load them into
  a temporary that is zeroed when out of bounds.
- Native texture atomic_store() returns void and takes the 4-component value before the coordinates:
  'u0.atomic_store(3u, 0u).x' ("member reference base type 'void'"). Emit
  'u0.atomic_store(uint4(value), coord)'.

Wrong bounds:
- The array stride was read from the element type (SPIRType::self of an array type is its element
  type), so arrays of structs used 4 bytes instead of their ArrayStride and the clamped index could
  reach 16 times past the end of the buffer. Look up the array type's own ArrayStride.
- A runtime array after a header ignored the member offset: bound it by (size - offset) / stride, as
  OpArrayLength does. Fixed-size arrays are bounded by their declared length too.
- The bounds check of an access chain was dropped after its first use, so a read-modify-write through
  one access chain stored out of bounds into the clamped last element. Keep it for every use.
